{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CggCW3e5RwWePfs4FtkXw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I don't have a ChEMBL/BindingDB/PubChem query tool in this session — no database, shell, or network access is available to me here, so I could not look this up. **The numbers below are from my own knowledge of the primary literature, not from a live database query**, and you should verify them against ChEMBL (imatinib = CHEMBL941; ABL1 = CHEMBL1862) before using them in a publication.\n\nImportant caveat on why \"the\" potency of imatinib for ABL1 is assay-dependent: imatinib is a type-II inhibitor that binds the DFG-out, inactive conformation. Its affinity therefore differs by orders of magnitude between the **non-phosphorylated (inactive)** and **activation-loop-phosphorylated (active)** kinase domain, and biochemical IC50 values also shift strongly with ATP concentration and substrate. Cellular values add membrane permeability and plasma-protein-binding effects.\n\nRepresentative values as I recall them:\n\n| Assay type | Value | Source (from recall) |\n|---|---|---|\n| In vitro kinase autophosphorylation (v-Abl / Bcr-Abl) | IC50 ≈ 25 nM (0.025 µM) | Druker et al., *Nat Med* 1996 (CGP57148B) |\n| Biochemical ABL1 kinase assay (purified enzyme, peptide substrate) | IC50 ≈ 0.1–0.6 µM, typical ~200 nM | Buchdunger/Manley-era Novartis enzyme assays; this is the range most ChEMBL IC50 entries fall in |\n| Binding affinity, **non-phosphorylated** ABL1 (competition binding, e.g. KINOMEscan) | Kd low nanomolar, ≲2 nM | Davis et al., *Nat Biotechnol* 2011 |\n| Binding affinity, **phosphorylated** ABL1 | Kd shifts up by ~2–3 log units (high nM to µM) | same conformational-selectivity literature |\n| Cellular proliferation, BCR-ABL–dependent cells (Ba/F3 p210, K562) | IC50 ≈ 260–680 nM | O'Hare et al., *Cancer Res* 2005 |\n\nConfidence: the ~25 nM autophosphorylation figure and the ~250–700 nM cellular range I am fairly confident about (these are heavily cited); the exact biochemical IC50 and the exact Kd digits I am less confident about and have given as ranges rather than invent a precise number.\n\nIf you want a single defensible number for a paper or a slide, pull the ChEMBL activity list for CHEMBL941 × CHEMBL1862 and report the median with the assay description, since the spread across ~dozens of deposited assays is real biology (conformational state), not noise.\n\n## ANSWER\n\nIC50 ≈ 25 nM against ABL1 in an **in vitro kinase (Bcr-Abl/v-Abl autophosphorylation) assay** (Druker et al., Nat Med 1996). Commonly quoted alternatives, same target: biochemical ABL1 **enzyme IC50 ~0.1–0.6 µM**; **cellular antiproliferative IC50 ~260–680 nM** in BCR-ABL–dependent cells; **binding Kd ≲2 nM** for non-phosphorylated ABL1, far weaker for the phosphorylated form.\n\nNote: recalled from literature, **not** retrieved — no database tool was available to me in this session."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18918,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18918},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f648b6fd-e368-420f-b294-66c7099fd5f0","uuid":"46d5d229-4365-4360-942d-ea480227c260","timestamp":"2026-11-03T15:04:29.620Z","request_id":"req_011CggCW3NDMkU7RkfGXsbvU"}
